16
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.291
(SIZZLING)

17
00:03:22.322 --> 00:03:23.715
MAN: Let down your hair.

18
00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.345
You do not pass for a boy.

19
00:03:31.848 --> 00:03:33.309
Let down your hair.

20
00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.480
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

21
00:03:44.763 --> 00:03:46.363
I am not afraid.

22
00:03:47.638 --> 00:03:49.637
Travelers spoke in these hills

23
00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:51.164
of the hero Robin Hood.

24
00:03:52.328 --> 00:03:53.745
He protects the meek.

25
00:04:01.761 --> 00:04:03.263
No one protects the meek.

26
00:04:05.382 --> 00:04:07.152
And he were no hero.

27
00:04:07.176 --> 00:04:10.613
He observed thrice a day
and gave silver to the poor.

28
00:04:10.637 --> 00:04:12.538
And he had the love
of Lady Marian.

29
00:04:12.563 --> 00:04:13.748
Is that not a hero?

30
00:04:13.773 --> 00:04:16.461
Lady Marian were taken
from an old shepherd's tale

31
00:04:16.486 --> 00:04:19.322
by a storyteller.
unfortunately. drunker than I.

32
00:04:20.481 --> 00:04:23.525
GIRL: She were the great love
of Robin Hood.

33
00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:25.319
ROBIN: Mmm.

34
00:04:26.862 --> 00:04:29.531
There is no great love
for one such as him.

35
00:04:32.936 --> 00:04:34.429
I have known
those who met this man.

36
00:04:34.453 --> 00:04:36.330
would you like to know a secret?

37
00:04:37.247 --> 00:04:38.290
I would.

38
00:04:39.872 --> 00:04:41.790
He never prayed once
in his life.

39
00:04:43.575 --> 00:04:46.220
These tales
spread across the land

40
00:04:46.245 --> 00:04:48.331
are lies upon lies.

41
00:04:49.671 --> 00:04:52.108
He were a murderous brigand.

42
00:04:52.239 --> 00:04:54.300
who perhaps cut the throat
of a sheriff

43
00:04:54.325 --> 00:04:56.470
in a long line of others.

44
00:04:56.495 --> 00:04:58.664
People saw a meaning
where there was none.

45
00:05:00.229 --> 00:05:02.124
He was not a hero.

46
00:05:02.148 --> 00:05:04.052
He robbed and killed
for the joy of it.

47
00:05:04.077 --> 00:05:05.328
nothing more.

48
00:05:10.801 --> 00:05:12.328
Those you met...

49
00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:15.787
did they say he felt remorse

50
00:05:15.812 --> 00:05:16.896
for his crimes?

51
00:05:20.909 --> 00:05:22.911
I suspect he felt tired.

52
00:05:26.118 --> 00:05:29.723
I would say if you met
this villain Robin Hood.

53
00:05:29.748 --> 00:05:31.102
you should make haste away

54
00:05:31.127 --> 00:05:33.337
and keep whatever life
you have left here.

55
00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:38.429
-(BREATHING HEAVILY)
-(CLOTHES FLUTTER)

56
00:05:40.947 --> 00:05:43.158
(FOOTSTEPS RECEDE)

57
00:05:44.197 --> 00:05:46.616
(MELLOW MUSIC PLAYING)

58
00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:06.658
(PRAYING INDISTINCTLY)
pardon of our sins.

59
00:06:08.394 --> 00:06:10.271
Through Jesus Christ our Lord.

60
00:06:11.775 --> 00:06:14.153
Amen. (BREATHING HEAVILY)

61
00:06:17.217 --> 00:06:19.386
(EERIE MUSIC PLAYING)

62
00:06:38.388 --> 00:06:40.557
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

63
00:07:08.419 --> 00:07:09.941
-(KNIFE SLASHING)
-(GIRL SCREAMING)

64
00:07:11.861 --> 00:07:13.822
(GIRL GURGLES)

65
00:07:17.114 --> 00:07:18.490
(GIRL CONTINUES GURGLING)

66
00:07:19.421 --> 00:07:21.697
Who was your kin?

67
00:07:23.968 --> 00:07:26.404
William Wainwright...
(INHALES SHARPLY)

68
00:07:26.429 --> 00:07:28.431
Of Pickering. (INHALES SHARPLY)

69
00:07:28.786 --> 00:07:30.162
Pickering?

70
00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:33.552
-Father?
-(GIRL WHEEZING)

71
00:07:33.577 --> 00:07:35.014
(GURGLING)

72
00:07:35.039 --> 00:07:36.290
ROBIN: Grandfather?

73
00:07:41.866 --> 00:07:44.042
You should have bathed.

74
00:07:44.067 --> 00:07:45.736
or waited for the wind
to change.

75
00:07:48.675 --> 00:07:50.761
Or kept your weight
on your back foot.

76
00:07:53.037 --> 00:07:54.538
-That's all.
-(GAGGING)

77
00:07:54.739 --> 00:07:55.990
You did well.

78
00:08:00.454 --> 00:08:02.622
(SOMBER MUSIC PLAYING)

79
00:08:02.763 --> 00:08:04.097
(FLESH SQUELCHES)

80
00:08:05.618 --> 00:08:06.912
(EXHALES SOFTLY)

81
00:08:12.295 --> 00:08:14.684
(WIND BLOWING)

82
00:08:14.709 --> 00:08:16.877
(SOMBER MUSIC CONTINUES PLAYING)

83
00:08:21.952 --> 00:08:24.162
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

84
00:08:37.837 --> 00:08:40.006
(SOMBER MUSIC CONTINUES PLAYING)

85
00:09:15.999 --> 00:09:17.142
(MUSIC FADES)

86
00:09:17.167 --> 00:09:18.628
WOMAN: They sing lies.

87
00:09:19.836 --> 00:09:21.588
These foolish common folk.

88
00:09:23.387 --> 00:09:26.140
Lies of his heroism
and goodness.

89
00:09:28.096 --> 00:09:29.640
But blood debts are owed.

90
00:09:31.256 --> 00:09:33.968
And generations
of his victims hunt him.

91
00:09:36.014 --> 00:09:37.223
For in truth...

92
00:09:38.832 --> 00:09:41.478
none were more wicked and wanton

93
00:09:41.503 --> 00:09:44.171
than the murderous bandit
Robin Hood...

94
00:09:45.918 --> 00:09:47.545
and his Little John.

95
00:09:51.797 --> 00:09:53.048
ROBIN: Speak to Thomas.

96
00:09:54.482 --> 00:09:57.044
He were hanged in the South.

97
00:09:57.401 --> 00:09:58.401
ROBIN: Will?

98
00:09:59.636 --> 00:10:01.055
They gutted him.

99
00:10:02.971 --> 00:10:04.598
I have no one else to ask.

100
00:10:06.873 --> 00:10:08.124
What happened?

101
00:10:09.016 --> 00:10:12.185
(SIGHS) My name is Edward.

102
00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:14.340
ROBIN: Who was Edward?

103
00:10:15.998 --> 00:10:19.270
I met him on the road.
ten summers ago.

104
00:10:19.295 --> 00:10:21.702
His brother died.
And he were going to grow

105
00:10:21.727 --> 00:10:23.771
rye and peas
on his brother's land.

106
00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:27.791
No one knew Edward's face.

107
00:10:29.381 --> 00:10:30.507
So I killed him.

108
00:10:31.661 --> 00:10:32.995
Now I am Edward.

109
00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:36.034
And Margaret's a good wife.

110
00:10:37.089 --> 00:10:38.341
It's a good farm.

111
00:10:39.318 --> 00:10:40.486
What happened?

112
00:10:41.845 --> 00:10:43.764
A family on the land
found me out.

113
00:10:44.572 --> 00:10:47.759
Took my farm. Took my Margaret.

114
00:10:47.784 --> 00:10:49.245
Tried to take my life.

115
00:10:52.948 --> 00:10:54.926
I cannot take back alone
what is mine.

116
00:10:54.951 --> 00:10:56.287
It's not yours.

117
00:10:58.428 --> 00:10:59.720
EDWARD: It's my family.

118
00:11:04.709 --> 00:11:06.045
I'm tired. John.

119
00:11:07.589 --> 00:11:09.008
Edward.

120
00:11:11.425 --> 00:11:12.801
I'm tired. Edward.

121
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:20.836
(SIGHS) It would be
a mighty battle.

122
00:11:21.308 --> 00:11:23.078
One for the stories.

123
00:11:29.239 --> 00:11:30.758
They are strong?

124
00:11:31.048 --> 00:11:32.049
Aye.

125
00:11:32.729 --> 00:11:34.874
They come from an old family.

126
00:11:34.899 --> 00:11:36.804
They say from Viking blood.

127
00:11:38.348 --> 00:11:40.660
There's a story
that once their elderfather

128
00:11:40.685 --> 00:11:41.686
fought a bear.

129
00:11:43.359 --> 00:11:45.945
It would be
a mighty battle. Robin.

130
00:11:48.852 --> 00:11:50.104
How many are there?

131
00:11:51.401 --> 00:11:53.361
EDWARD: Four? Five?

132
00:11:53.995 --> 00:11:55.580
Together we can best them.

133
00:11:58.975 --> 00:12:00.785
We'll likely die.

134
